<GuidePage
  slug="how-to-play-tambola"
  description="Learn how to play tambola (housie): how tickets are laid out, how numbers are called, early five, lines, corners and full house, checking claims and bogeys."
  intro="Tambola is one of the easiest party games to learn and one of the hardest to stop playing. This guide covers everything a first-time host or player needs, from what is on a ticket to settling a disputed claim."
>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>What is tambola?</h2>
    <p>
      Tambola, also called <strong>housie</strong> or <strong>housie-housie</strong>, is a numbers game played with
      tickets and a caller. The caller draws numbers from 1 to 90 at random and reads them out. Players mark the called
      numbers on their tickets, and the first person to complete an agreed pattern, such as a full row, wins that prize.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a close cousin of 90-ball bingo, which is played in the UK. In India it has been a favourite at kitty
      parties, housing society events, Diwali and New Year gatherings, weddings, school fairs and family get-togethers
      for decades. It works just as well over a video call. Because the only skill needed is paying attention,
      grandparents and ten-year-olds can play at the same table on equal terms.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>One ticket per player</strong> (players can hold two or three if they can keep up). You can print a set
        with our <a href="/tambola-ticket-generator/">tambola ticket generator</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A way to call numbers.</strong> The traditional kit is a box of 90 numbered tokens. A phone works just as
        well: our <a href="/tambola-number-caller/">tambola number caller</a> draws each number at random, reads it aloud
        and keeps a board of everything called.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Pens or markers</strong> for every player, unless you play on phones.</li>
      <li><strong>A list of prizes</strong>, agreed before the first number is called.</li>
      <li><strong>A host</strong> who calls numbers, checks claims and keeps things moving.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>How a tambola ticket works</h2>
    <p>Every standard tambola ticket follows the same layout, which is what makes the game fair:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>It has <strong>3 rows and 9 columns</strong>, so 27 boxes in all.</li>
      <li>
        Exactly <strong>15 boxes hold numbers</strong>, 5 in each row. The other 12 are blank.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each column holds numbers from one band: the first column has 1 to 9, the second 10 to 19, the third 20 to 29,
        and so on. The last column holds 80 to 90, so it has eleven possible numbers instead of ten.
      </li>
      <li>Every column has at least one number, and numbers in a column go from smallest at the top to largest at the bottom.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Tickets are often printed in <strong>sheets of six</strong>. A proper sheet uses all 90 numbers exactly once
      across its six tickets, so whoever holds a full sheet marks a number on every single call. That is why some
      players buy a whole sheet at bigger events.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
      <strong>Tip:</strong> handwritten or randomly made-up tickets often break these rules, for example with two
      numbers from the same row band or a row with six numbers. That makes some tickets more likely to win than
      others. A generator that follows the layout rules keeps every ticket on equal terms.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>How to play, step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Hand out tickets.</strong> Give each player a ticket and something to mark it with. If you print
        numbered tickets, note who has which number so you can check claims quickly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Announce the prizes.</strong> Say out loud which patterns win, in what order they are usually claimed,
        and what each prize is. Write them somewhere everyone can see.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Agree the house rules.</strong> The two that matter most are when a claim must be made (see below) and
        what happens after a wrong claim.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Start calling.</strong> The caller draws one number at a time, reads it clearly, often with its
        traditional nickname, and gives players a few seconds to find it before the next one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Players mark numbers.</strong> If a called number is on your ticket, mark it. If not, wait for the next
        call.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claim a prize.</strong> As soon as you complete a pattern, shout something agreed in advance, such as
        "Housie!", "Claim!" or the name of the prize. Calling stops while the host checks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the claim.</strong> The host reads out the numbers on the claimed pattern and confirms each one
        has been called. If they all have, the prize is won.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Carry on until the full house.</strong> Prizes that have been won are crossed off. The game ends when
        someone completes the whole ticket.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

