  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Tambola prizes and patterns</h2>
    <p>These are the classic prizes that almost every game includes:</p>
    <h3>Early five (Jaldi five)</h3>
    <p>
      The first player to mark any five numbers on their ticket wins. The numbers can be anywhere. With a room full of
      tickets it is usually claimed in the first 10 to 20 calls, so it gets everyone excited early. Some groups play early seven instead.
    </p>
    <h3>Top line, middle line and bottom line</h3>
    <p>
      All five numbers in one row. Each line is a separate prize, so up to three people can win a line. Lines are
      usually won in the middle part of the game.
    </p>
    <h3>Four corners</h3>
    <p>
      The first and last numbers of the top row and the first and last numbers of the bottom row. "First" and "last"
      means the leftmost and rightmost numbers, not the corners of the grid, which are often blank.
    </p>
    <h3>Full house</h3>
    <p>
      All 15 numbers on the ticket. This is the big prize and ends the game. Some hosts add a second full house, which
      keeps the game going for one more winner.
    </p>
    <h3>Popular extra patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Star:</strong> the four corners plus the middle number of the middle row.</li>
      <li><strong>Pyramid:</strong> the middle number of the top row, three numbers in the middle row and all five in the bottom row.</li>
      <li><strong>Breakfast, lunch and dinner:</strong> all numbers in columns 1 to 3, columns 4 to 6, or columns 7 to 9.</li>
      <li><strong>Odd or even:</strong> every odd (or even) number on the ticket.</li>
      <li><strong>Lucky number:</strong> the host secretly picks a number before the game. Whoever has it on their ticket when it is called wins a small prize.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For more twists, see our <a href="/guides/kitty-party-tambola-ideas/">kitty party tambola ideas</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Claims, checks and bogeys</h2>
    <h3>When does a claim count?</h3>
    <p>
      The most common house rule is that you must claim <strong>before the next number is called</strong>. If you
      notice your line was complete two numbers ago and someone else has already claimed it, you have missed it. This
      rewards players who pay attention and stops arguments about who finished first.
    </p>
    <h3>What if two people claim at once?</h3>
    <p>
      If both claims are correct and were completed on the same number, the usual answer is to share the prize. If the
      prize can't be split, toss a coin or let a <a href="/spin-the-wheel/">spin wheel</a> decide.
    </p>
    <h3>What is a bogey?</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>bogey</strong> is a wrong claim: the player has marked a number that was not called, or misread their
      pattern. Groups handle it differently. In friendly games the player is just told which number is missing and play
      continues. In stricter games the ticket is out for that prize, or for the rest of the game. Decide before you
      start so nobody feels singled out.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you host with tickets on phones, our caller's room mode checks each claim against the numbers actually called,
      so a bogey is caught without anyone reading out a ticket.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Tips for a great game</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Call at a steady pace.</strong> Around 8 to 12 seconds per number suits most groups. Slow down for young
        children or older relatives, and speed up for experienced players.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Repeat each number.</strong> "Seven, lucky seven, number seven" saves a lot of "What was that?".
        Traditional nicknames help, and our <a href="/guides/tambola-number-calls/">list of tambola calls 1 to 90</a> has
        all of them.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Keep a visible board.</strong> A screen showing called numbers makes checking easy and settles disputes.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep prizes small and fun.</strong> Chocolates, small gifts or a funny trophy are plenty. Tambola is a game for fun, not money.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan for 20 to 40 minutes.</strong> With 15 to 30 tickets in play, the full house usually comes somewhere around the 65th to 80th call. Fewer tickets means a longer game.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel faq">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Is tambola the same as bingo?</summary>
      <p>
        It is very close to 90-ball bingo. Both use numbers 1 to 90 and 3-row tickets with 15 numbers. The names and the
        favourite prizes differ: bingo halls usually play one line, two lines and full house, while tambola adds early
        five, corners and many fun patterns.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How many people can play?</summary>
      <p>
        Any number from three upwards. With fewer than five players, give everyone two tickets so prizes don't take too
        long. Big events can have hundreds of players, as long as each ticket is unique.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can two tickets be the same?</summary>
      <p>
        They should not be. Duplicate tickets mean two people win every prize together. A good generator, including
        ours, makes every ticket different.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can we play tambola online?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. Share your screen on a video call while you run the caller, and send everyone a ticket or a room link. The
        caller page explains how to do this.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
